// ADR 0075 D3 (register D87): a handler arm is a LOOP BODY, so ADR 0036 D8's
// loop-carried move rule applies to it.
//
// The `handle`'s dispatch loop re-enters an arm for every operation the handled
// computation performs, and a `k(v)` whose resume bubbles branches straight back into
// it — but the borrow checker walks the arm once, exactly as it walks a `while` body
// once. `two()` performs twice, so this arm runs twice: the first entry moves `outer`
// into `inner`, and the second reads `outer` again, after it was consumed.
//
// `MovedInLoopBody` has rejected the same shape around a `while` since ADR 0036 D8;
// this is that rule one construct over, and it is what makes ADR 0075 D1's per-entry
// drain of the arm's scopes sound — the drain is only correct while nothing an arm
// binding owns outlives the entry that bound it.
//
// Conservative in the same way the loop rule is: it does not ask whether the arm is
// in fact entered twice, because that is a property of the handled computation at run
// time. An arm that resumes a `handle` body performing exactly once is rejected here
// too. The fix is the same as for a loop — bind inside the arm, or borrow.
//
// The field spelling (`bag.items`, ADR 0046's partial move) is rejected by the ROOT of
// the moved place, so it is covered as well; this fixture pins the whole-binding form,
// and `sentinel-borrow-check`'s unit tests cover the field one.

effect Io {
    read() -> i64;
}

fn two() -> i64 ! { Io } {
    let a: i64 = perform Io.read();
    let b: i64 = perform Io.read();
    a + b
}

fn f() -> i64 {
    let outer: [i64] = [21, 1, 2];
    handle two() with {
        Io.read(k) => { let inner: [i64] = outer; k(inner[0]) }
    }
}

fn main() -> i64 {
    f()
}
